---
class: title
# THE ANGEL NEXT DOOR <br>
by Paul Slade Smith <br> 
<em>adapted from Ferenc Molnár’s play At the Castle</em>
---

## CHARLOTTE:
And ask yourself… 

---

## OLIVER: 
Yes?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
How does it begin?
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh no—please.
---

## OLIVER:
I like that!
---

## ARTHUR:
No—let’s not—
---

## CHARLOTTE:
This room, for instance.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh God.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Look at this room, as we step into it.
---

## OLIVER:
Alright.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What if—instead of a room—
---

## ARTHUR:
Here it comes—
---

## CHARLOTTE:
This were a stage, a set.
---

## OLIVER:
Yes!
---

## ARTHUR:
No—
---

## CHARLOTTE:
The three of us walk in—
---

## OLIVER:
The characters!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes!
---

## ARTHUR:
No—please don’t encourage her.
---

## OLIVER:
Oh, I think it’s fun.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Three characters, making their first entrance.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
And the challenge for the playwright is: 

---

You can’t start with narration to set the scene; it’s not a novel. You only have dialogue.

---

## OLIVER:
Of course!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
So these people walk in, and the audience asks: Who are they? Where are they? Why are they there? What are you looking for?
---

## ARTHUR:
Is that the audience asking, or you?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Arthur.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh! She said my name. Now the audience knows who I am.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Arthur likes to be disagreeable.
---

## ARTHUR:
And now they know why I’m here! Aha! Found the liquor!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I think that’s why you’re here.
---

## OLIVER:
Is this the way the two of you write your plays?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What—by bickering?
---

## ARTHUR:
That’s not a bad idea. Maybe they’d run longer.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Don’t kid yourself; we bickered plenty writing No Noodles For Nancy!
---

## ARTHUR:
And yet…
---

## CHARLOTTE:
And yet…
---

## OLIVER:
And yet what? That’s the one on Broadway, right? The one that just…opened?
---

## ARTHUR:
He doesn’t know?
---

## OLIVER:
What? What don’t I know?
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh Christ, now we’re actually in a play.
---

## ARTHUR:
A piece of information integral to the plot is inexplicably unknown by one of the characters, causing the other two to “fill him in,” purely for the sake of the audience.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
That’s not the audience, darling.
---

## ARTHUR:
I beg your pardon?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
If this is a stage, the audience is out there.
---

## ARTHUR:
What? No. That can’t be the fourth wall, you’d lose those gorgeous windows.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well, that can’t be the fourth wall, we’d lose two doors.
---

## ARTHUR:
This room could stand to lose a couple of doors. There are too many doors in this room.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
That’s what makes it like a set!
---

## ARTHUR:
That door doesn’t even open!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes—which makes it like a set!
---

## OLIVER:
Okay, honestly—you really ought to write your plays this way.
---

## ARTHUR:
What we ought to do is what all theatre people ought to do, which is, for the love of God, stop talking about the theatre.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Shall we talk about novels instead?
---

## ARTHUR:
Yes! Capital idea!
---

## OLIVER:
I think we should talk about this room. Or this view, for heaven’s sake.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
It is something. Even at night, when you can’t see the ocean.
---

## OLIVER:
Can you imagine living here, in a mansion?—would you call this a mansion?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I believe the millionairess who owns it calls it her cottage.
---

## ARTHUR:
God, I hate the rich. Except when they invite us to their mansions and let us drink all their liquor.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I’m not sure all the liquor was part of the invitation.
---

## ARTHUR:
It was implied. She’s a patron of the arts; we’re artists; artists drink. Charlie.
---

## ARTHUR:
And speaking of drinking—and artists—I would like to propose a toast.
---

## ARTHUR:
To the man of the hour—an extraordinary young writer—and his remarkable achievement. To Oliver.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
To Oliver!
---

## OLIVER:
And also… CHARLOTTE. Yes?
---

## OLIVER:
To the audience finally knowing my name.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Soon enough, young man, the whole country will know your name.
---

## OLIVER:
Oh.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I mean it. That book is gonna sell.
---

## ARTHUR:
Like hotcakes.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
It’s gonna be a sensation.
---

## ARTHUR:
I predict rave reviews and boffo sales—and all of it well deserved, because—despite your complete lack of ego—you are a great novelist.
---

## OLIVER:
Well…
---

## ARTHUR:
What?
---

## OLIVER:
I’m not sure one novel makes one a novelist.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Of course it does! Especially a published novel.
---

## OLIVER:
Well…
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What?
---

## OLIVER:
Not published yet.
---

## ARTHUR:
Yes, alright, but Simon & Schuster handed you a contract this very morning, signed and sealed.
---

## OLIVER:
Well… ARTHUR and CHARLOTTE. What?
---

## OLIVER:
Not entirely signed.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What does that mean?
---

## ARTHUR:
Who didn’t sign it—Simon or Schuster?
---

## OLIVER:
No, I didn’t… I haven’t signed the contract. I told them I’d come back Monday.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What the hell are you talking about?
---

## ARTHUR:
Ah, to be young and idiotic again.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You told us you’d signed with a publisher. We came here so you could tell your girlfriend you’d signed with a publisher.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh! Now the audience knows why we’re here.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
It’s why we drove to Newport.
---

## ARTHUR:
And now they know where we are!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
That is not the audience! Oliver. Is there a reason you turned down a life-altering sum of money and a chance at literary fame?
---

## OLIVER:
I… I—look—I, I didn’t mean to mislead you. I know there are many things riding on my signing that contract. Your play, for instance.
---

## ARTHUR:
For instance?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Arthur.
---

## ARTHUR:
Our play, my boy, is not a parenthetical. Writing a stage adaptation of your book took us many, many months— OLIVER. I know—
---

## ARTHUR:
Many unremunerated months— CHARLOTTE. He knows—
---

## OLIVER:
And your play is perfect, and I want to see it produced, and I want to see my book published, but before any of that can happen… I have to talk to Margot.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Talk to Margot?
---

## OLIVER:
Yes.
---

## ARTHUR:
You mean…your girlfriend, Margot? The one who’s staying here in the same mansion we’re staying in?
---

## OLIVER:
Yes.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well…Ollie, that’s the easiest thing in the world.
---

## ARTHUR:
No—it isn’t.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Of course it is. You could go talk to her now, except—
---

## ARTHUR:
There’s a complication.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
A minor complication: She’s “out for the evening,” apparently, but as soon as she’s back—
---

## ARTHUR:
No, there’s something he’s not telling us: 

---

The reason he suddenly decided last week we should drive up here to see Margot in her concert tomorrow—but then decided today it couldn’t wait till tomorrow, 

---

we had to come tonight—and then insisted we surprise her and not say we were coming early.
---

## ARTHUR:
What’s going on? What are you keeping from us?
---

## OLIVER:
You’re going to think I’m ridiculous.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Ollie. What is it?
---

## OLIVER:
If I’m embarrassed to tell you, it’s because the two of you are so mature, and…successful and…established.
---

## ARTHUR:
Old, I think, is the word you’re searching for.
---

## OLIVER:
No—no—you’re inspirational.
---

## OLIVER:
And generous—when I think of all you’ve given me, the way you’ve encouraged my writ- ing—just because you’re friends of my parents—
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Dear friends of your dear parents—
---

## OLIVER:
But you welcomed me to New York, you introduced me to a world of playwrights and poets and actors. None of this would have happened without you.
---

## OLIVER:
Not even The Angel Next Door, because… would I ever have had the idea for my novel if it hadn’t been for that party at your apartment?
---

## OLIVER:
The night I met Margot? But as much as I have you to thank…this wouldn’t have happened without her. 

---

Without the inspiration she…ignited in me.
---

## OLIVER:
Her voice, her…face, her…everything I saw in her that night and have dreamed about since that night. 

---

This novel is Margot, Margot is this novel.
---

## OLIVER:
But… CHARLOTTE and ARTHUR. But?
---

## OLIVER:
She hasn’t read it. CHARLOTTE and ARTHUR. What?
---

## OLIVER:
I’ve been afraid to let her. What if she doesn’t like it? What if I’ve captured her wrong?
---

## OLIVER:
Margot is…perfection, she is an angel—as ideal a woman as the heavens have ever created.
---

## ARTHUR:
She hasn’t read…any of it?
---

## OLIVER:
She has no idea I’ve been writing it.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Writing that? Those three hundred and seventeen pages?
---

## ARTHUR:
What does she think you do all day?
---

## OLIVER:
Well, we don’t see each other that often.
---

## OLIVER:
She’s always busy in her world—Broadway and touring and giving concerts for millionaires in mansions—and I’ve been…in my tiny apartment, working on this.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well now: You’re both here—together—for a whole weekend.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You’ll talk to Margot, you’ll let her read this masterpiece, for heaven’s sake—she’ll be head over heels for it.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
And then…you can sign that contract, yes?
---

## ARTHUR:
And then…we can get our play produced, yes?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Exactly. Which is not an incidental thing.
---

## OLIVER:
No, I know.
---

## ARTHUR:
It’s kind of how we make a living.
---

## OLIVER:
I understand.
---

## ARTHUR:
When we make a living. Which, at present, we’re not exactly doing.
---

## OLIVER:
I am sorry I didn’t know about your new show closing.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh, we’ve forgotten all about that play.
---

## ARTHUR:
As did everyone who saw it, apparently.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
All we’re focused on now is our next play—our adaptation of your beautiful book, starring Margot Bell as The Angel Next—wait.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You have told Margot we’ve written this role for her, haven’t you?
---

## OLIVER:
Not exactly.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh! Amazing!
---

## OLIVER:
Well, since I didn’t tell her about the book, I could hardly tell her about the play.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
We’ve been talking to producers, Ollie. We’ve built a whole show around her.
---

## ARTHUR:
We’ve timed her entrance applause.
---

## OLIVER:
Margot gets entrance applause?
---

## ARTHUR:
Not if she doesn’t know she’s in the play.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Alright, no more secrets, no more being coy, you’ll tell her everything tonight—as soon as she gets back from wherever she is.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You just listen for her—she’s staying in the very next room; you’ll probably hear her the moment she comes in.
---

## OLIVER:
Margot’s staying right there?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes! I arranged that specially, when I discovered the bedroom next to hers was available. Isn’t it romantic?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Your bedroom and Margot’s bedroom—separated only by a wall? It’s like Pyramus and Thisbe.
---

## ARTHUR:
That wall.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes. That wall. Which is why…
---

## ARTHUR:
That can’t be the audience.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
And, young man, not only do you and your angel share a wall, you also share… a door.
---

## OLIVER:
A door…between our bedrooms?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes! I mean, not that you have to…
---

## OLIVER:
Oh!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I’m sorry, I thought the two of you were… OLIVER. Oh!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Not that you should be… or shouldn’t be… I mean…
---

## OLIVER:
I…don’t think I would ever
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh—no—I didn’t…
---

## OLIVER:
And I hardly think Margot would ever…
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh—she—no—that’s…
---

## ARTHUR:
Sooner or later, one of you is going to finish a sentence.
---

## OLIVER:
Margot’s not like some young women these days, she is…beauty and purity. It’s why I call her an angel in the book.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Naturally. We’re just gonna forget all about that door.
---

## ARTHUR:
Didn’t I say there were too many doors in this room?
---

## OLIVER:
But I do think, before she comes back… I haven’t seen her in forever, and, after that long drive from Manhattan, I’m a bit… I think I’ll take a bath.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh!
---

## OLIVER:
Would you mind terribly staying in here—in case I miss her coming in?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Not at all. You go make yourself presentable. And we will ring for a bottle of champagne, how does that sound?
---

## OLIVER:
Absurdly extravagant—and lovely. Though it is after midnight. Is that too late?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
The housekeeper specifically said—no matter the hour—we should feel free to ring the kitchen staff downstairs.
---

## ARTHUR:
Goodness, it’s just like being at home—if we had a housekeeper, or a kitchen staff, or a downstairs.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
We’ll order champagne and light refreshments, and have it all delivered by the time you’ve emerged from your bath.
---

## OLIVER:
Charlotte. Arthur. I’m so happy we came here this weekend. I’m happy for your friendship. I’m happy for… so many things.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
So are we, Ollie.
---

## ARTHUR:
I’m happy just seeing you so happy.
---

## OLIVER:
I am. I am…unreservedly, unabashedly happy.
---

## ARTHUR:
We are well and truly screwed.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What the hell is going on?
---

## ARTHUR:
This is definitely ending horribly.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I’m so confused.
---

## ARTHUR:
I need another drink.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I can’t believe—
---

## ARTHUR:
Our play will never get produced?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I don’t understand—
---

## ARTHUR:
How we’re going to pay our rent?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Will you stop— ARTHUR. Always being right?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You can’t claim you were right. You didn’t know— ARTHUR. Oliver was a virgin?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well—no—but, no, you didn’t know that either. Did you?
---

## ARTHUR:
Of course not. I thought they were lovers. I thought they had to be.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Exactly, given everything we know about young people these days.
---

## ARTHUR:
It’s not young people in general we’re talking about. We’re talking about Margot.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Arthur.
---

## ARTHUR:
I am not disapproving! I am all for sex and people having it.
---

## ARTHUR:
I am merely saying…to hear Oliver talk about her—“Margot’s not like other women, she’s an angel, untouched by human hands—” 

---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes, well—
---

## ARTHUR:
I’m pretty sure Margot has been touched by human hands.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Arthur.
---

## ARTHUR:
Do they even know each other?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What?
---

## ARTHUR:
Have we ever seen them together?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
They met at our party.
---

## ARTHUR:
Eighteen months ago!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I spoke to Margot last week—when she arranged these rooms for us. When I told her we were coming to the concert— with Oliver.
---

## ARTHUR:
Did she say “Oliver who?”
---

## CHARLOTTE:
They’re in love, Arthur. You’ve read his novel.
---

## ARTHUR:
Yes, I have. But she hasn’t! What if…what if that book is a complete fiction? Hmm? 

---

What if he met her a year and a half ago and hasn’t seen her since?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Why are you having a fit about whom Oliver is dating?
---

## ARTHUR:
Because he hasn’t signed that contract!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What does that have to do with—?
---

## ARTHUR:
Our agreement with Oliver was—
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh for God’s sake, I know what our agreement was—
---

## ARTHUR:
We don’t have the rights to do our play— CHARLOTTE. Yes, yes, yes—
---

## ARTHUR:
Until his book is published.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You think I don’t know that?! 

---

Did you not see me start mentally selling off our furniture when he pulled that unsigned paper out of his bag?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
But you heard him: He just needs to show Margot his novel, so let him!
---

## ARTHUR:
He thinks she’s an angel.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
He’s a romantic.
---

## ARTHUR:
What happens when he finds out she’s not?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What is that?
---

## ARTHUR:
A clue. Discovered downstairs moments after we entered the building.
---

## ARTHUR:
While you were otherwise engaged with the house staff, I wandered into a dimly lit room off the grand entrance, 

---

and there, upon a small table, I came upon: a program.
---

## ARTHUR:
A program for tomorrow's concert. The concert in the ballroom. The concert Margot is singing in.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
If this monologue doesn’t end up solving a murder, you’re really overplaying it.
---

## ARTHUR:
Look! “An Evening of Songs and Scenes, featuring Margot Bell and Victor Pratt.” You remember Victor.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Vaguely.
---

## ARTHUR:
Handsome fella? Matinee idol? Broadway’s Favorite Baritone?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Who calls him that?
---

## ARTHUR:
He does. He put it in the program.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
God, what an idiot.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh, he’s always been an idiot, but very popular with the ladies.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well, idiots often are.
---

## ARTHUR:
Dated every young actress on Broadway—usually more than one at a time—including— 

---

## CHARLOTTE:
Including— 

---

## ARTHUR and CHARLOTTE:
Margot.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
So? That was years ago. Now you see their names in a program and conclude, what? They’re having sex?
---

## ARTHUR:
Yes!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You think Margot’s cheating on Oliver?
---

## ARTHUR:
I think she doesn’t know Oliver exists.
---

## ARTHUR:
But she definitely doesn’t know he’s here tonight, and she doesn’t know we’re here tonight, and Oliver doesn’t know Victor’s here at all.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well when you put it that way.
---

## ARTHUR:
Yes, it sounds like a tragedy.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No, it sounds like a farce.
---

## ARTHUR:
Do you honestly believe this story ends happily?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Don’t I always?
---

## ARTHUR:
You think Oliver signs that contract?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes—on Monday morning.
---

## ARTHUR:
And our play gets produced?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
On Broadway, starring Margot Bell.
---

## ARTHUR:
Alright. Just wanted it on the record.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well, what do you believe?
---

## ARTHUR:
What do I always believe?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
That Death will come calling for us all?
---

## ARTHUR:
You mock me, but don’t say I didn’t warn you.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
When Death comes calling?
---

## ARTHUR:
Who’s that?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I rang for champagne, remember? Before you predicted the apocalypse?
---

## OLGA:
Hello. How you do.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Ah! Um…Olga, is it? The housekeeper?
---

## OLGA:
Olga, yes.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
We met downstairs.
---

## OLGA:
Yes. I give you room key.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes.
---

## OLGA:
To your room.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Thank you.
---

## OLGA:
Not this room. This room not your room.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh! No, we’re, uh… We’re visiting our friend.
---

## OLGA:
You visit friend?
---

## ARTHUR:
Yes.
---

## OLGA:
Why he no here?
---

## ARTHUR:
He just… He went… He’s in the bathtub.
---

## OLGA:
Theatre people.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I beg your pardon?
---

## OLGA:
You are theatre people, yes?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
We’re—yes, we’re playwrights.
---

## OLGA:
Always with the happy-happy, always with the kiss-kiss, always with the friend in tub.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I’m not sure what you’re suggesting.
---

## OLGA:
I suggest nothing. I state fact! But…forgive me, is very late, I forget: 

---

You are guest, I am servant, you ring bell, I am here, what you want?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh! Well…if it’s not too much bother… we were wondering if the kitchen could provide us with a bottle of champagne.
---

## OLGA:
Champagne? You throw party?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No, it’s just a…small, romantic reunion.
---

## OLGA:
With friend in tub?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No, we’re not—I mean… 

---

## ARTHUR:
He’s not always in the tub.
---

## OLGA:
This not helping story. But I bring champagne. Is good? Okay, good.
---

## ARTHUR:
Could we…add a little something to eat, as well?
---

## OLGA:
Why not? What else kitchen staff have to do in middle of night?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I thought you told us to ring at any hour.
---

## OLGA:
I say that to all guests. Only theatre people believe me. 

---

But, no worry, I bring sandwiches. Is good? Okay, good.
---

## ARTHUR:
Wait! Olga—there is one more question we’d like to ask.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
There is?
---

## ARTHUR:
Miss Margot Bell—her room is next door, yes?
---

## OLGA:
You know Miss Margot?
---

## ARTHUR:
She’s a friend. We’ve come to see her concert.
---

## OLGA:
Ah—concert, yes. With Victor Pratt.
---

## ARTHUR:
Exactly.
---

## OLGA:
Broadway’s Favorite Baritone.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Are you kidding me?
---

## ARTHUR:
Right, and we were wondering if you know where Margot is tonight.
---

## OLGA:
Who is asking?
---

## ARTHUR:
Well…we are asking.
---

## OLGA:
Perhaps I not at liberty to divulge.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
We’re only wondering if Margot and Victor…are together.
---

## OLGA:
Together? Margot and Victor? Ha! When they not together?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh?
---

## OLGA:
Those two always together. Together in his room. Together in her room.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh boy.
---

## OLGA:
Everyone know when they together. You can hear them through wall.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I beg your pardon?
---

## OLGA:
When they doing it. They so loud. You hear everywhere.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
When they’re…? You don’t mean…?
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh, I think she does mean… 

---

## CHARLOTTE:
I thought maybe she meant… 

---

## OLGA:
What?
