## OLGA:
You crazy.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No—no, you say “Good morning!” Thereby establishing the passage of time.
---

## OLGA:
You don’t know it’s morning?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No, I know it’s morning.
---

## OLGA:
Why you still up? You stay up all night.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh that works too!
---

## OLGA:
Ha?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
“Why you still up, you stay up all night?” also establishes the passage of time.
---

## OLGA:
You crazy.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Not crazy. On a mission. Frantically typing all night in a desperate attempt to salvage our play—our livelihoods, really. 

---

Wait—is that crazy?
---

## OLGA:
I make living by bringing breakfast to crazy people. That crazy.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You’re right. You win. Well, thank you for breakfast.
---

## OLGA:
You no eat?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I haven’t time, I’m afraid.
---

## OLGA:
You must eat. Start with fruit. Is cut. Is fresh. Is freshly cut.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Thank you; I’m both touched and terrified. But you see, my mission is only half done.
---

## OLGA:
You not like other guests. 

---

These friends and relatives who stay here—they eat, they sleep, they drink, they relax. I despise them.
---

## OLGA:
But you…woman on mission… 

---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes?
---

## OLGA:
What is mission?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Um… Changing people’s lives through the power of theatre?
---

## OLGA:
Ah! Doomed to fail. Completely hopeless. 

---

I mean, you go down and you go down hard. But good on you for trying.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Wait. Weren’t you about to say something supportive?
---

## OLGA:
Eat your fruit.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Good morning. Is it possible to connect me to another bedroom? 

---

Where Miss Margot Bell is staying. Thank you so much.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Good God, the phone is right there. Oh! She’s not kidding about that fruit.
---

## MARGOT:
Hello?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Hello, Margot?
---

## MARGOT:
I’m sorry, who is this?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Charlotte Sanders, dear. Sorry to wake you—
---

## MARGOT:
Charlotte? Oh my goodness—
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I know it’s early—
---

## MARGOT:
That’s alright—where are you calling from?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You’ll never guess. Newport. We came a day early.
---

## MARGOT:
You’re here?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
And wouldn’t you know it, I’m in the bedroom next to yours.
---

## MARGOT:
What?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I’m in the bedroom next to yours.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh that’s funny, I think I can hear you through the wall.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes, it is funny, isn’t it. Though it gets less funny over time.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Listen, dear, there’s something rather delicate I need to share with you—something I’d rather not say over the phone.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Could I ask you to come over here?
---

## MARGOT:
Oh—of course!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
There’s a door that connects our rooms—it’s to your left.
---

## MARGOT:
To my…left. Wait. How did you know which way I was facing?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Practice. Just come over, will you?
---

## MARGOT:
I’ll be right there. Charlotte—my goodness—it’s been ages!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Look at you—half asleep and still gorgeous.
---

## MARGOT:
I thought you weren’t coming till tonight. When did you get in?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Midnight?
---

## MARGOT:
Well, I wish I’d known you were right next door!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes, you do, you really do. And it’s not just me and Arthur here; we also brought Oliver.
---

## MARGOT:
I know! Sweet Oliver. I’m… looking forward to seeing him. 

---

That is… Wait, you know, don’t you. I can see it on your face.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Know what?
---

## MARGOT:
What are you playing at? You know that Oliver and I have this… Oh, I don’t even know how to describe it. 

---

We’ve only met once—at your party.
---

## MARGOT:
And ever since, we’ve had this…well clearly he’s told you.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I’d like to hear you tell it.
---

## MARGOT:
It’s kind of a romance of letters. We’re lovers…on paper. 

---

He writes me beautiful letters, and poems, and I—less poetically—write him back.
---

## MARGOT:
It’s incredibly sweet. Only…once you’d told me he was coming to see me… 

---

I guess it means admitting none of it was real.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Wasn’t it real?
---

## MARGOT:
Well, it couldn’t be; we never see each other. 

---

And the woman he’s writing to… I mean, I’m not exactly an “angel,” am I.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Margot, I have something to tell you.
---

## MARGOT:
What? What is it?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Why don’t you have a seat?
---

## MARGOT:
Is it Oliver? Has something happened?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Just sit, please.
---

## MARGOT:
Why should I sit?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
So when your knees collapse under you, you won’t have as far to fall. 

---

I told you we arrived around midnight, in this room.
---

## MARGOT:
Yes?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You were on a boat at that time.
---

## MARGOT:
How do you know that?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You can’t look shocked yet, dear, or you’ll have nowhere to go. 

---

You came back to your room, singing… what was the song? Yes—you sounded heavenly.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, you heard that? Through the wall?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Here is where you should start to panic. 

---

You were then joined in your room by a man.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh God.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Victor Pratt, Broadway’s Favorite— 

---

## MARGOT:
Jesus.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Now that’s worth putting on a poster.
---

## MARGOT:
You mean…you heard… 

---

## CHARLOTTE:
Every word.
---

## MARGOT:
No.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Every kiss.
---

## MARGOT:
No!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Every ode composed to your lllips, your lllips, the lllips he missed, the most kissable lllips—
---

## MARGOT:
Would you please stop talking!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Exactly the prayer I was making last night.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, I’m mortified! I mean, it’s bad enough you heard me with a man, but…Victor Pratt?! 

---

God! Why did I start things up again with that idiot?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
It’s very simple dear; you missed…his body.
---

## MARGOT:
What?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Touching his body, feeling your skin against his skin—
---

## MARGOT:
Oh no!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Running your hands through his hair, touching him—
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, I think I’m going to be sick.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
It certainly had that effect on me. And not only me. 

---

When I say we were in this room last night.
---

## MARGOT:
You mean Arthur too.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes, me, and Arthur, and…
---

## MARGOT:
Oh God. Oliver?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Your romantic poet.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh that’s horrible!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
More horrible than you realize. Because this romance you’ve been carrying on was, to him, very real. 

---

That angel he’s been writing to?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
He thought she existed.
---

## MARGOT:
He didn’t.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Of course he did. Because she was writing him back.
---

## MARGOT:
My letters!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Your very romantic letters.
---

## MARGOT:
I was just… I was writing to him what he was writing to me.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
But, you see, he meant it. And now, I’m afraid, you’ve broken his heart.
---

## MARGOT:
No, no, I never meant to… I mean, that night we met, I did feel… Oh God, what have I done?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
There’s one more thing you need to see: the love letter he didn’t send you.
---

## MARGOT:
The Angel Next Door?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
His first novel. He doesn’t know I’m giving it to you. 

---

And once he finds it, he’s going to tear it into bite-sized pieces.
---

## MARGOT:
What?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
A gorgeous book, which the world will never read.
---

## MARGOT:
Why are you telling me this?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I need to know: If there were something you could do…to undo what happened last night…would you do it?
---

## MARGOT:
Well—yes, in an instant.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Good.
---

## MARGOT:
What are you doing?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Could you connect me to the bedroom of Victor Pratt?
---

## MARGOT:
Oh God—wait!—do we have to have him this morning?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
We only have to have him this morning, dear, because you had him last night. 

---

Hello, Victor? Oh dear, did I wake you?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
It’s Charlotte Sanders calling—from a room down the hall, would you believe. Charlotte Sanders.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes, Arthur Sanders’ wife—silly of me to leave that part out.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Listen, Victor, I’ll get straight to the point: 

---

I know where you were last night at two in the morning, and whom you were with.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
And I know a certain young lady in New York who would be very upset to hear about it.
---

## MARGOT:
What?!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Just a guess. Ah! Turns out I was right. 

---

There’s no point in denying last night, Victor. I have proof.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Now I need you to come immediately to the bedroom next to Margot’s. No more arguing, Victor. 

---

Come at once, or I’m placing a call to Manhattan.
---

## MARGOT:
You’re telling me he has a girlfriend?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Do you know any Bettys on Broadway?
---

## MARGOT:
I know a bunch of Bettys on Broadway.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Which one’s not the brightest Betty?
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, clearly I’m not the brightest Betty. 

---

Good God, how stupid am I? Is this what happens to every ingénue?
---

## MARGOT:
Do we all end up as foolish and gullible as the roles we play?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Maybe it’s time you started playing better roles.
---

## MARGOT:
You’re the playwright; you want to write me one?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I already have. In a play based on that book. 

---

If we survive this day, I’ll tell you all about her.
---

## VICTOR:
Now look here, Charlotte—I don’t know what you think you heard last night, but it wasn’t… Margot.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh, it was definitely Margot. Well, Margot and you.
---

## MARGOT:
Don’t even look at me, you swine.
---

## VICTOR:
Wait. What’s happening?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Why don’t you have a seat?
---

## VICTOR:
What did she tell you?
---

## MARGOT:
Just sit down.
---

## VICTOR:
Why is everyone telling me to sit?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
So when your knees collapse under you— 

---

## MARGOT:
Oh let him fall, it’ll be fun to watch.
---

## VICTOR:
Listen, you’re both making me a little nervous.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
That’s a good sign; it means you’re not a complete idiot. Victor Pratt: You confessed you have a girlfriend.
---

## VICTOR:
I did not! Did I? I mean… I’m not saying I don’t have a girlfriend.
---

## MARGOT:
Good God, you can’t even stay faithful through an entire sentence.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yet last night you entered the bedroom of Margot Bell—
---

## VICTOR:
With a key! She gave me her key!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You’re not denying you were in there?
---

## VICTOR:
Oh! Right! How dare you suggest such a thing!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I take it back. You are a complete idiot.
---

## VICTOR:
Okay, so I went to her room. We’re doing a concert together. Perhaps I was there to…
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Recite poetry?
---

## VICTOR:
Sure! Maybe.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
About the most kissable lllips you ever…kissed?
---

## VICTOR:
What?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Lllips so soft, but oh. So. Hot?
---

## VICTOR:
How do you…?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
About how much you’ve wanted to be here again, with her?
---

## VICTOR:
How do you know the thoughts that were in my brain?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
They weren’t in your brain; you said them out loud.
---

## VICTOR:
How do you know the thoughts I said out loud?
---

## MARGOT:
She heard us, you idiot. She heard us talking, through that wall.
---

## VICTOR:
Oh! Wall! Look, I… I take it all back.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Which part?
---

## VICTOR:
That part! I don’t want to be with her! I never liked her. I can’t stand the sight of her!
---

## MARGOT:
Do you think this is helping you with anyone in this room?
---

## VICTOR:
I’m just saying—I’m begging you…please don’t tell Betty.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, nice.
---

## VICTOR:
It will kill her.
---

## MARGOT:
How much do we have to tell Betty for her to kill you?
---

## VICTOR:
I mean it; she’s a sensitive soul—
---

## MARGOT:
So that’s why you cheated on her, you lying, little, two-timing—?
---

## VICTOR:
Wait, why are you mad that I cheated on—
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Both of you: Sit. Down.
---

## VICTOR:
Why do I have the feeling this is going to get worse?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
The thing is, Victor: I’m not the only one who heard the two of you last night. 

---

A young man named Oliver Adams was also in this room.
---

## VICTOR:
Is he…a murderer? 

---

## MARGOT and CHARLOTTE:
What?
---

## VICTOR:
I don’t know—why does everyone look terrified?
---

## MARGOT:
He’s just a sweet young man. Who…happens to be in love with me.
---

## VICTOR:
Oh. Oh!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
And he’s traumatized by everything he heard.
---

## VICTOR:
But he’s…he’s not going to kill me?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No, but unless you want Betty to hear everything Oliver heard, I need you to agree—right now—that you’ll help us in a little scheme.
---

## VICTOR:
I will! I’ll do it! What’s a scheme?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What’s the scheme?
---

## VICTOR:
No—what’s a scheme?
---

## MARGOT:
Just let her talk.
---

## VICTOR:
Right.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
First of all: What happened in that room last night—
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, please, please—we all know what happened.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No, we don’t. That’s the point. What happened in that room was not the passionate lovemaking of two misguided idiots.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
It was two actors, rehearsing a play.
---

## VICTOR:
What two actors?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You two actors! You were rehearsing. Running lines. You were acting.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh my God! Oh, that’s brilliant!
---

## VICTOR:
Um…
---

## MARGOT:
Don’t you get it?
---

## VICTOR:
I will. I’ll get it.
---

## MARGOT:
Charlotte, that’s perfect! But…wait.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What?
---

## MARGOT:
Oliver’s no fool. He’s not going to believe those words are from a play when that play doesn’t exist.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well, lucky for us… that play does exist.
---

## MARGOT:
What?
---

## VICTOR:
What?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
This is the play you were rehearsing last night.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Containing all the dialogue we heard through that wall, and some dialogue invented by the playwright.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
True, it did not exist last night, but by six o’clock this morning, I finished what is truly the most ridiculous and yet most consequential play I have ever written.
---

## VICTOR:
Oh. Oh!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Margot, you’re playing the role of “Anna,” Victor is “Albert,” and Arthur and I will play the supporting roles.
---

## MARGOT:
Wait, we’re performing this?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
We have to! The only way to convince Oliver what he heard last night was a play is for him to see that play. Tonight.
---

## VICTOR:
Tonight?
---

## MARGOT:
As part of our concert.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Exactly.
---

## MARGOT:
Charlotte, you’re a genius!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
But listen: No one can know I wrote it. 

---

Not even Arthur; he’s the worst liar in the world.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
So this script is not new, it’s… I don’t know, it’s an old Hungarian play, recently translated into English.
---

## VICTOR:
That sounds like a bit of a stretch.
---

## MARGOT:
But how do we explain you and Arthur being in it?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You found out this morning two actors had left you in the lurch, so you asked Arthur and me to step in and save the day.
---

## MARGOT:
You’ve thought of everything.
---

## VICTOR:
Wait, wait, hold on—you’ve got…every word we spoke last night in this script.
---

## VICTOR:
I’m supposed to repeat all my passionate lovemaking in front of strangers?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Think of it as acting, Victor. It’s not you saying those words, it’s your character.
---

## MARGOT:
Yes, just act like you mean what you’re saying, and the audience will believe you. Like I did last night.
---

## VICTOR:
That’s not fair. I meant it last night.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, don’t start lying about your lying.
---

## VICTOR:
I wasn’t lying.
---

## MARGOT:
Shall we tell that to Betty?
---

## VICTOR:
Now don’t you bring Betty into this—
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, I’d like to bring Betty into this—
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Stop it, stop it! You are not allowed to kill each other until after the play. 

---

Now, concentrate.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You’ve got a lot of lines to learn by eight o’clock tonight. 

---

Make that seven o’clock. That’s when the four of us are meeting in here to rehearse.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Understood? Good, now go get to work. But whatever you do: Do not rehearse in there.
---

## VICTOR:
Oh! Because—right!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No, wait, you can’t go out that door; someone might see you leaving. 

---

And if anyone asks: You haven’t seen me.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
And you and I spoke only briefly this morning. 

---

You handed me these. And I handed you this.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh. Thank you.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
At one point, Oliver did want you to read it. 

---

Maybe, if this works, he’ll want that again.
---

## VICTOR:
I haven’t seen you? Am I seeing you now?
---

## ARTHUR:
Charlotte?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
That’s Arthur. Quickly! Go!
---

## ARTHUR:
Charlotte?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Good morning!
---

## ARTHUR:
Are you being ironic?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No, I’ve got a plan, I’ve worked it all out, but first: How is Oliver?
---

## ARTHUR:
How do you think he is? He’s a wreck; he didn’t sleep. 

---

I didn’t either; I’ve been valiantly guarding a valise that has nothing in it.
---

## ARTHUR:
He’s determined to destroy that manuscript, Charlie—he’s not joking. 

---

And we may as well do the same to our play; it’s never getting a production.
---

## ARTHUR:
Wait, someone brought you breakfast. Nobody brought me breakfast.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well I’m glad you’re making up for it by eating mine.
---

## ARTHUR:
Where is the manuscript? Have you hidden it?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I gave it to Margot.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh, okay, you—wait—what?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I told you I had a plan. That’s half of it. Here’s the other half.
---

## ARTHUR:
What’s this?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
That’s your scene from the play we’re performing tonight.
---

## ARTHUR:
My scene from the…
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I’m playing “Katalin, the lady’s maid,” you’re playing God. 

---

It’s a perfect role for you!
---

## ARTHUR:
Are you having a stroke?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What?
---

## ARTHUR:
Am I having a stroke? Why are the words you’re saying not making any sense?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Sorry, I should start from the beginning. Margot called me this morning. From next door.
---

## ARTHUR:
She called you?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes.
---

## ARTHUR:
On the phone, or through the wall?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Listen to me: She heard from Olga that we came up a day early, and it turns out—
---

## ARTHUR:
Did you, did you tell her what we heard last night?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Of course not, why would I do that?—but listen: 

---

It just so happens that she and Victor need a couple of actors for this play they’re doing tonight, 

---

and she was wondering if you and I would step in, so I said yes!
---

## ARTHUR:
Are you having a stroke?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Would you stop with the—?
---

## ARTHUR:
Why would you say yes? Why are we doing a play? How the hell does that help anything?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Now we have to stay for the concert. And so does Oliver.
---

## ARTHUR:
So?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
It’s the perfect way for Oliver and Margot to have more time together.
---

## ARTHUR:
He doesn’t want more time with her, Charlotte, he wants to have never met her. 

---

He’s apoplectic knowing they’re both in the same house.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Trust me. All we have to do is get him to that concert tonight and everything will be fine.
---

## ARTHUR:
This is your brilliant plan? This is why you’re so happy?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes—plus: I get to see you act in a play!
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh now you’re being funny. You hate my acting.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I do not!
---

## ARTHUR:
You think I’m awkward. And wooden.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I never said wooden.
---

## ARTHUR:
So, God, huh? I’m not great at memorizing. 

---

Am I supposed to learn this by tonight?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You’re only in two pages.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh that’s why you said it’s the perfect role for me.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
No, it’s because you’re playing God.
---

## ARTHUR:
You’d think God could have negotiated himself a bigger role.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oliver! Oh, my dear boy! How are you doing?
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh, I didn’t mention, he’s gone through quite a range of emotions since last night.
---

## ARTHUR:
From violent sobbing, to quiet weeping, to angry mumbling, to…this.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What’s this?
---

## ARTHUR:
Complete silence, except for… the sighing.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh, Ollie, I know the world seems impossibly cruel right now, but…things will get better soon, I promise.
---

## ARTHUR:
That’s literally all you’re going to get.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Would you…like some breakfast? How about a pancake—I’m sure there’s one or two Arthur hasn’t eaten yet.
---

## ARTHUR:
Um…
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Seriously? Look, Ollie, I know you’re feeling…angry. 

---

Well, disillusioned, perhaps. I guess I mean…betrayed.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Sorry, I shouldn’t try to guess what you’re feeling.
---

## OLIVER:
Stupid. I’m feeling stupid. 

---

