## VICTOR:
You know what I’ve been thinking about? Your lllips.
---

## MARGOT:
My lllips?
---

## VICTOR:
I’ve missed those lllips. The most kissable lllips I ever kissed.
---

## ARTHUR:
Impossible!
---

## OLIVER:
Right?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What are you talking about?
---

## ARTHUR:
How do you not hear it? Listen! Listen to the scene. It sounds remarkably like something we heard very recently.
---

## VICTOR:
Um… Hello? Actors. Acting.
---

## ARTHUR:
Our apologies—please. Continue: from, uh, “your lllips, my lllips.”
---

## VICTOR:
Your lllips.
---

## MARGOT:
My lllips?
---

## VICTOR:
I’ve missed those lllips. The most kissable lllips I ever kissed.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh!
---

## MARGOT:
That’s almost poetry.
---

## VICTOR:
Our last kiss. Do you remember?
---

## MARGOT:
Like it just happened.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh! Oh, I see.
---

## VICTOR:
Your lllips so soft. But… oh. So. Hot. ARTHUR and OLIVER. Oh. So. Hot.
---

## OLGA:
Do you mind? Some of us…are trying to watch the show.
---

## ARTHUR:
They were rehearsing. Last night! They were running lines!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh! Is that what it is?
---

## OLIVER:
How did you not figure that out? It’s the only explanation!
---

## MARGOT:
Is there any way we could possibly…?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes. We’re sorry. We will let you finish, and—we will not overreact, whatever emotions we may be feeling as we’re hearing this dialogue.
---

## OLGA:
Yes, whatever emotions we feeling, we keep inside.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Olga, are you okay?
---

## OLGA:
Okay, good.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Please. Proceed. From, uh…“Your lips so soft…”
---

## VICTOR:
Your lllips so soft. But oh. So. Hot.
---

## MARGOT:
If only I could give you another kiss like that right now.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh!
---

## OLIVER:
That’s what they were doing?
---

## ARTHUR:
All those times they weren’t talking, they were—?
---

## VICTOR:
Could we please?
---

## OLIVER:
Sorry, I’m so sorry.
---

## MARGOT:
What—is it ridiculous?
---

## OLGA:
Ridiculous? No! Is so moving. They wish to touch. They cannot touch! Is beautiful, but sad!
---

## OLIVER:
She’s right. It’s making me understand things that I… Please…continue.
---

## VICTOR:
Listen. I know that time has passed. Too much time. But do you ever think about the nights we had together?
---

## ARTHUR:
How did we not know this was dialogue?
---

## VICTOR:
Making love—sweet, passionate love!—from midnight till dawn?
---

## ARTHUR:
I mean it’s so obvious. No one talks like that.
---

## VICTOR:
I beg your pardon?
---

## ARTHUR:
It’s so fake. Everything you’re saying.
---

## OLGA:
You know why? I think he lying to her.
---

## VICTOR:
What?
---

## MARGOT:
Like he’s someone who can’t be trusted?
---

## OLGA:
Exactly.
---

## MARGOT:
Like he’s got a girlfriend in Heaven he’s not telling her about?
---

## VICTOR:
Now hold on.
---

## ARTHUR:
It’s not you, Victor. It’s just your insipid dialogue.
---

## OLIVER:
Well I, for one, would not change a word of it. I love this play more every time I hear it. Please—keep going.
---

## VICTOR:
I…don’t remember where we were.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
“Making love—” EVERYONE BUT VICTOR. “Sweet passionate love! From midnight till dawn.”
---

## VICTOR:
Right. Thank you. Do you know how long I’ve wanted to be here again—with you?
---

## MARGOT:
You know that can’t happen.
---

## OLIVER:
Oh! Of course!
---

## MARGOT:
We can never be together.
---

## ARTHUR:
’Cause he’s dead!
---

## VICTOR:
But…don’t you miss this? Us?
---

## MARGOT:
Yes. I miss…your body.
---

## OLIVER:
Oh!
---

## MARGOT:
I miss…touching your body.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh! Right!
---

## MARGOT:
Running my hands through your hair. Feeling you…there.
---

## ARTHUR:
His ear?
---

## MARGOT:
Feeling you…here.
---

## ARTHUR:
His nose?
---

## OLIVER:
That would not have been my guess.
---

## MARGOT:
Feeling you…
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh! God! ALL. What?!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Look at the time! We have to get downstairs!
---

## OLGA:
No!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
These two have to get ready for the concert. The guests will already be arriving.
---

## OLGA:
You cannot stop! I must know how it end. What happen to Anna? What happen to Albert?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
You’ll have to come to the ballroom and find out.
---

## OLGA:
I will. I must! Is all theatre as good as this?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I’d like to think some theatre might even be better.
---

## OLGA:
Impossible. Is so compelling, so full of surprises.
---

## ARTHUR:
It was indeed. The best surprise I’ve have ever had at the theatre.
---

## OLIVER:
Margot, I… I don’t even know where to begin.
---

## MARGOT:
That seems like a very good place to begin.
---

## OLIVER:
This play…
---

## MARGOT:
Was it horrible?
---

## OLIVER:
It’s quite possibly my favorite thing in the world.
---

## VICTOR:
What’s this now?
---

## OLIVER:
Victor!
---

## VICTOR:
You…liked our performance?
---

## OLIVER:
I liked that it was a performance. That was the best part.
---

## VICTOR:
I think it could use a little more rehearsal.
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh! No! No more rehearsing! This play is harmless in performance, but nearly lethal when rehearsed.
---

## OLIVER:
I’m sorry if…earlier I was…
---

## MARGOT:
Maybe we can start by neither of us apologizing for any misunderstandings? Or misperceptions. Or imperfections.
---

## OLIVER:
I should have called you. Long ago.
---

## MARGOT:
That sounds like an apology.
---

## OLIVER:
Instead of writing poems.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, I cherish those poems!
---

## OLIVER:
Margot, if I…quickly dress for the concert…could I… would you be my…?
---

## MARGOT:
Yes. Go. Yes.
---

## OLIVER:
Arthur! Find a safe place for this, would you? I’ve got to get it to Simon & Schuster first thing Monday morning.
---

## ARTHUR:
And our play?
---

## OLIVER:
Let’s take it all the way to Broadway.
---

## ARTHUR:
Thank God! Oh! That’s me!
---

## MARGOT:
Well.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
I feel like that…worked?
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, completely. I think we’ve been given a fresh start. I’m so grateful. I only wonder…
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Yes?
---

## MARGOT:
He doesn’t still think I’m an angel, does he?
---

## ARTHUR:
I heard the word “angel”—are we talking about the play? You do know about the play, yes? The one you’re starring in?
---

## VICTOR:
Is this the play you’re taking to Broadway? I don’t suppose there’s anything for me in it—a handsome lover, perhaps, who’s gone to Heaven?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh, I’m sure we could kill off one of the characters.
---

## ARTHUR:
For you, Victor, we’ll kill him off before he even enters!
---

## VICTOR:
Oh. Thank you.
---

## OLGA:
I have read to ending. Is a triumph!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Olga, I’m touched you were affected by our little play.
---

## OLGA:
What was mission you say you were on? Changing lives through theatre?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Something like that. Do you think I might succeed?
---

## OLGA:
Yes, damn you. Your theatre has changed my life. So happy, so stupid. I love it. I love it! Oh God!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What?
---

## OLGA:
I am theatre people. I am theatre people!
---

## MARGOT:
Well, I believe I have a concert to get to. And a date.
---

## ARTHUR:
With Oliver?
---

## MARGOT:
Yes—It’s been quite a consequential weekend.
---

## ARTHUR:
It certainly has.
---

## MARGOT:
I don’t know how any of this ends, but… CHARLOTTE. Do we ever? Go. We want to hear you sing romantic love songs.
---

## MARGOT:
Oh, I get to do that, don’t I? Aren’t I the lucky one.
---

## ARTHUR:
Well, Charlie, we have done it.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oh, we have, have we?
---

## ARTHUR:
Yes! We’ve reached the denouement!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Ah!
---

## ARTHUR:
Lovers reunited, confusions untangled, theatrical rights to a novel secured!
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Are you saying you’re happy…with a happy ending?
---

## ARTHUR:
I am over the moon! And: insanely, immeasurably in awe of you. I mean… When the hell did you write that play?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
When did you figure out I wrote it?
---

## ARTHUR:
Well, it should have been the sixteenth or seventeenth line about Victor being an idiot.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Too much?
---

## ARTHUR:
Exactly the right amount of too much. You saved a young man’s heart tonight. And a great American novel.
---

## ARTHUR:
And our only source of income for the next twelve months.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Oliver! I thought we’d see you downstairs. Aren’t you late for a date?
---

## OLIVER:
How?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
What?
---

## OLIVER:
How did you…?
---

## ARTHUR:
Oh dear.
---

## OLIVER:
You don’t have to tell me, just know that… I know what you did today, and… I know what they did last night, and… I know how ridiculous I’ve been.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Ollie—
---

## OLIVER:
And how lucky I am that you… wrote a play for me.
---

## OLIVER:
There’s a remarkable, beautiful woman waiting for me downstairs, so… Just know that I’m grateful, and embarrassed, and so…so happy we came here this weekend.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well. Hasn’t this been fun?
---

## ARTHUR:
It has quite literally been the most stressful twentyfour hours of my life.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Ah! Conflict. There’s a play in there somewhere.
---

## ARTHUR:
Yes. Set in the bedroom of a Newport mansion with one too many doors. Wait—where did we decide the audience is?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Out there.
---

## ARTHUR:
What do you suppose they’re thinking right now?
---

## CHARLOTTE:
They’re wondering if the rest of the cast is waiting behind these doors, ready to come out for the curtain call.
---

## ARTHUR:
Nope. I guess we’re not in a play, my dear.
---

## CHARLOTTE:
Well…it was fun to pretend.

---
